WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.905
Container scanner output can be noisy.

2
00:00:03.985 --> 00:00:08.731
A single image may include operating
system packages,

3
00:00:08.811 --> 00:00:16.446
language dependencies, application
libraries, and inherited base image components.

4
00:00:16.526 --> 00:00:23.233
Scanner severity is useful, but severity
alone does not determine priority.

5
00:00:23.313 --> 00:00:28.781
The reviewer should consider
exploitability, reachability,

6
00:00:28.861 --> 00:00:36.084
package ownership, workload exposure,
available fixes, compensating controls,

7
00:00:36.164 --> 00:00:40.910
and whether the finding applies to the
deployed image.

8
00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:43.673
Reachable components matter.

9
00:00:43.753 --> 00:00:50.769
A vulnerable package that is installed but
unused may carry different risk from a

10
00:00:50.849 --> 00:00:57.452
vulnerable library directly exposed
through an internet-facing feature.

11
00:00:57.532 --> 00:01:03.516
False positives can occur because scanners
infer package versions,

12
00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:08.652
rely on imperfect metadata, or lack
application context.

13
00:01:08.732 --> 00:01:16.161
Triage turns raw findings into prioritized
work the development or platform team can

14
00:01:16.241 --> 00:01:16.860
act on.

15
00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:21.480
Stale images are a common source of repeat
findings.

16
00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:28.782
If an image has not been rebuilt against
current base images or dependency updates,

17
00:01:28.862 --> 00:01:35.569
the vulnerability count may grow even when
application code has not changed.

18
00:01:35.649 --> 00:01:43.078
Patching may require updating base images,
rebuilding, validating compatibility,

19
00:01:43.158 --> 00:01:48.110
and promoting the new image through the
release process.

20
00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:55.722
The remediation path should reflect how
the organization actually ships containers.

21
00:01:55.802 --> 00:02:01.580
AI can help summarize and prioritize
findings for analyst review.

22
00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:07.025
It can group vulnerabilities by base
image, package manager,

23
00:02:07.105 --> 00:02:11.852
application dependency, or likely
remediation path.

24
00:02:11.932 --> 00:02:18.741
It can draft questions for package owners
or turn scanner output into a clearer

25
00:02:18.821 --> 00:02:20.060
triage table.

26
00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:26.433
Those summaries are useful only after the
reviewer checks the evidence,

27
00:02:26.513 --> 00:02:33.220
confirms context, and avoids treating
model output as a final risk decision.
